Countries of the World: Exploratory Data Analysis & Demographic Modeling
Project Overview
An end-to-end Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) and statistical modeling project executed in Python (Jupyter Notebook), analyzing global demographic, geographic, and economic indicators across 227 countries. This project investigates structural socio-economic relationships, regional disparities in birth/death rates, and global population concentration patterns.

Key Performance Indicators & Dataset Scope
Dataset Scale: 227 countries and 20 multi-dimensional socio-economic and geographical features.

Core Metrics Tracked: Population density, net migration, infant mortality, GDP per capita, literacy rates, phone penetration, and regional demographic shares.

Data Cleansing & Preprocessing: Handled specialized comma-decimal separators, managed missing values across heterogeneous data types, and engineered structural feature transformations via Pandas.

Core Visualizations & Analytical Layout
Correlation Matrix Heatmap: Evaluates multi-collinearity and statistical dependencies across numeric indicators like GDP, literacy, infant mortality, and population metrics using Seaborn.

Regional Distribution Boxplots: Visualizes the spread and outlier distribution of GDP per capita across distinct global regions.

Clustered Column Charts: Contrasts regional birth rates versus death rates side-by-side to highlight demographic transitions.

Stacked Bar Charts: Models the population concentration of top global superpowers (e.g., China, India, United States) against the rest of the world.

Scatter Plots with Hue Segregation: Maps GDP per capita against literacy rates, segmented by geographic region to analyze developmental correlations.

Technical Stack & Libraries
Language: Python

Data Manipulation & Analysis: NumPy, Pandas

Data Visualization: Matplotlib, Seaborn

Environment: Jupyter Notebook (Anaconda environment)
